#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 1500.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222397} :activity/id #uuid "2774bc0e-42a3-5956-a4b7-a719151ea7c2" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049288 :topic/id #uuid "ea0735e9-7712-5a1c-a626-25cab33f54cb" :topic/math-academy-id 6386} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222397 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222522 :tutorial/content "Suppose a linear ODE has a periodic forcing term whose Fourier series is known, for example\n\n$$\ny' + ky = f(x),\n\\qquad\\text{where}\\qquad\nf(x) \\sim \\frac{c_0}{2} + \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\Big(c_n\\cos(\\omega_n x) + d_n\\sin(\\omega_n x)\\Big).\n$$\n\nA standard strategy is to look for a *particular* solution with the *same Fourier frequencies*:\n\n$$\ny_p \\sim \\frac{a_0}{2} + \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\Big(a_n\\cos(\\omega_n x) + b_n\\sin(\\omega_n x)\\Big)\n$$\n\nThen, the method includes the following steps:\n\n- **Step 1:** Differentiate term-by-term.\n\nUsing $\\dfrac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\cos(\\omega_n x)=-\\omega_n\\sin(\\omega_n x)$ and $\\dfrac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\sin(\\omega_n x)=\\omega_n\\cos(\\omega_n x)$, we get\n\n$$\ny_p' \\sim \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\Big(-a_n\\omega_n\\sin(\\omega_n x) + b_n\\omega_n\\cos(\\omega_n x)\\Big).\n$$\n\n- **Step 2:** Substitute into the ODE and collect like terms\n\nThen, we plug $y_p$ and $y_p'$ into the equation, and rewrite the left-hand side as\n\n$$\n\\Big(\\substack{\\large\\text{free} \\[3pt] \\large\\text{term}}\\Big) \\;+\\; \n\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\Big(\\substack{\\large\\text{cosine} \\[3pt] \\large\\text{coefficient}}\\Big) \\cos(\\omega_n x) \\;+\\; \n\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\Big(\\substack{\\large\\text{sine} \\[3pt] \\large\\text{coefficient}}\\Big) \\sin(\\omega_n x).\n$$\n\n- **Step 3:** Equate coefficients.\n\nBecause the Fourier series representation is unique (up to convergence issues we ignore here), we match:\n\n- the free terms, and\n\n- for each $n\\ge 1$, the cosine coefficients and sine coefficients.\n\nThis produces:\n\n- one equation for $a_0$ (from the constant term), and\n\n- a $2\\times 2$ linear system for $(a_n,b_n)$ for each $n\\ge 1$.\n\n- **Step 4:** Write the Fourier series of $y_p.$\n\nAfter solving for $a_0$, $a_n$, and $b_n$, substitute them back into\n\n$$\ny_p \\sim \\frac{a_0}{2} + \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\Big(a_n\\cos(\\omega_n x) + b_n\\sin(\\omega_n x)\\Big).\n$$\n\nLet's see some examples." :tutorial/id #uuid "84328521-7170-5404-8ec2-a41475c2ad15" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14711 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "0521dca3-2fe6-50aa-8dc9-282e35ffe718" :step/math-academy-id 607546 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222398}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222398 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221455 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221203 :question/id #uuid "03fbfe2c-b12f-5220-9fae-e0dfe210a505" :question/math-academy-id "e-21857"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4733fab7-18ca-5336-9aba-53212ddbf87b" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049370} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050010}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222031} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222032} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317443} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186397662} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186397676} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512930} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551258} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551307}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving First Order ODEs Given the Fourier Series of the Forcing Function"} :step/id #uuid "ab947003-2e2f-5620-996d-e8070699c2a1" :step/math-academy-id 607547 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222399}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222399 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222523 :tutorial/content "For second-order linear ODEs, the overall algorithm is the same as for first-order equations: \n\n- we assume a Fourier series for $y_p$,\n\n- substitute into the ODE, and\n\n- match coefficients.\n\nBefore we start, we can use the forcing series properties to simplify our work. For a second-order equation of the form \n\n$$\ny'' + \\omega^2 y = f(x)\n$$\n\nwith no $y'$ (damping) term, the particular solution can be simplified.\n\nThe key insight comes from the fact that the differential operator \n\n$$\nL[y] = y'' + \\omega^2 y\n$$\n\nmaps cosines to cosines and sines to sines. This means we can match the form of our assumed solution $y_p$ to the form of the forcing function $f(x),$ which saves a great deal of work.\n\nThis leads to some powerful shortcuts:\n\n- If $f(x)$ is a *pure cosine series* (an even function), we can assume $y_p$ is also a pure cosine series (all $b_n=0$).\n\n- If $f(x)$ is a *pure sine series* (an odd function), we can assume $y_p$ is also a pure sine series (all $a_n=0$, including $a_0=0$).\n\n- If the constant term in the series for $f(x)$ is zero, the constant term in $y_p$ will also be zero (so $a_0=0$).\n\nLet's see an example." :tutorial/id #uuid "12f51f58-a298-5fc1-9540-6d9f4611481b" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14712 :tutorial/title "Second Order ODEs With Fourier Series"} :step/id #uuid "d1b21edf-14d7-5094-a313-f622ad12149b" :step/math-academy-id 607550 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222403}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222400 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221456 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221204 :question/id #uuid "3944f6e4-a3a8-5bad-87c5-18227ce223bc" :question/math-academy-id "e-21858"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5e39d476-7dcb-5213-a461-df7bd1919b9f" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050378}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222035} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222037} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317447} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317448} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317449} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317450} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368331} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368337} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484247} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484267} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507450} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507482} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512900} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524971} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186525016}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving Second Order ODEs Given the Fourier Series of the Forcing Function"} :step/id #uuid "2fb61d84-6e73-5592-aa15-0582c4b7b2f2" :step/math-academy-id 607551 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222401}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222401 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222528 :tutorial/content "A forcing harmonic can cause *resonance* when its frequency matches the natural frequency of the homogeneous equation.\n\nIn cases like that, we proceed as follows:\n\n- **Step 1:** Find the natural frequency.\n\nFor an equation of the form\n\n$$\ny''+\\omega^2 y = f(x),\n$$\n\nthe homogeneous solution oscillates with natural frequency $\\omega$.\n\n- **Step 2:** Find the forcing frequencies.\n\nIf $f$ is $2L$-periodic, then its Fourier series uses harmonics\n\n$$\n\\cos\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{L}\\right),\\qquad \\sin\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{L}\\right),\n$$\n\nwhose frequencies are\n\n$$\n\\omega_n=\\frac{n\\pi}{L}\\qquad(n\\ge 1).\n$$\n\nA harmonic is *resonant* exactly when $\\omega_n=\\omega$.\n\n- **Step 3:** Form the particular solution ansatz for each harmonic.\n\n- For a *non-resonant* harmonic ($\\omega_n\\ne \\omega$), we use the standard ansatz:\n\n$$\ny_{p_n}=A\\cos(\\omega_n x)+B\\sin(\\omega_n x)\n$$\n\n- For a *resonant* harmonic ($\\omega_n=\\omega$), this form overlaps with the homogeneous solution. We must use the *modified ansatz* by multiplying by $x{:}$\n\n$$\ny_{p_n}=x\\left(A\\cos(\\omega x)+B\\sin(\\omega x)\\right)\n$$\n\nThis extra factor of $x$ produces a new trial function that can match the forcing term.\n\nWhen $f$ is given as a Fourier series, we check each harmonic:\n\n- *non-resonant* harmonics contribute ordinary sine/cosine terms to $y_p,$ while\n\n- *resonant* harmonics contribute terms multiplied by $x.$\n\nIn particular, the resonant harmonic is the one with index $n$ satisfying\n\n$$\n\\frac{n\\pi}{L}=\\omega.\n$$\n\nLet's see how this works in practice." :tutorial/id #uuid "1aff3ba6-cd76-5ae7-bcd6-d05e9fb44694" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14776 :tutorial/title "Cases Where the Forcing Term Induces Resonance"} :step/id #uuid "6d7e21fe-4716-5031-8d15-1ac9a8196ed5" :step/math-academy-id 607555 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222402}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222402 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221457 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221206 :question/id #uuid "926606c2-1e1a-5aab-8f2e-826163a204a2" :question/math-academy-id "e-21875"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9e42e65b-6e26-5a3f-9275-25a291e13138" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048966}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222039} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222040} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317451} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317452} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317453} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368351} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368362} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186397690} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186397701} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186397712} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507448} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507508} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524966} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551352}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Resonant Harmonics"} :step/id #uuid "ca55a627-e4f5-543f-96b1-6632f3610f35" :step/math-academy-id 607556} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222403 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222530 :tutorial/content "Consider the differential equation $y''-4y=f(x),$ where the Fourier series of the $2$-periodic forcing function is given by\n\n$$\nf(x) \\sim \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{2}{n^2}\\cos(n\\pi x).\n$$\n\nBecause $f$ has no free term and is a pure cosine series, we assume\n\n$$\ny_p \\sim \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty a_n\\cos(n\\pi x).\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & ∼\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x))∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-{a}_{n}n\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x) \\\\ {y}_{p}^{″} & ∼\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-{a}_{n}n\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x))∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-{a}_{n}{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $y''_p$ and $y_p$ into the equation and have\n\n$$\n\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty -a_n n^2\\pi^2\\cos(n\\pi x)\n-\n4\\left(\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty a_n\\cos(n\\pi x)\\right)\n=\n\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{2}{n^2}\\cos(n\\pi x).\n$$\n\nRearranging the terms, we get\n\n$$\n\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty -a_n(n^2\\pi^2+4)\\cos(n\\pi x)\n=\n\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{2}{n^2}\\cos(n\\pi x).\n$$\n\nNow, equating the cosine coefficients, we obtain\n\n$$\n-a_n(n^2\\pi^2+4)=\\dfrac{2}{n^2}\n\\qquad\\Rightarrow\\qquad\na_n=-\\dfrac{2}{n^2(n^2\\pi^2+4)}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, a Fourier series for a particular solution is\n\n$$\ny_p \\sim \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\: -\\dfrac{2}{n^2(n^2\\pi^2+4)} \\cdot \\cos(n\\pi x).\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "f2d6d8f9-00d1-552b-889a-e54ed8d22d21" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14873 :tutorial/title "A Worked Example of Solving a Second Order ODE"} :step/id #uuid "483f5b2d-f9cb-5272-a15f-759e449ff9c0" :step/math-academy-id 607559 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222400}}] :activity/title "Solving ODEs Using Fourier Series" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186221475}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046660} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484357} :activity/id #uuid "2aeb0302-967f-5ddb-8bf1-16281ba96227" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049288 :topic/id #uuid "ea0735e9-7712-5a1c-a626-25cab33f54cb" :topic/math-academy-id 6386} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484314 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317453 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0fe6e1ef-2a02-59be-967b-360af6440b14" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "n=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315213} {:answer/id #uuid "2493fd79-af96-5964-b4d5-79c55216b0d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "n=4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315324} {:answer/id #uuid "acf5a8df-8734-5bf9-9bcc-688e3e570e08" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "n=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316092} {:answer/id #uuid "ae1a6513-a24b-5275-9db3-0cd9e31e4d3d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "n=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316104}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315213} :answer-field/id #uuid "6b3c4e4e-5751-5f8c-b537-c9c21b1bec65" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316808} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "113bb42a-cce4-59ec-8300-8cfaa0e4ff29" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{x}{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315221} {:answer/id #uuid "514af182-9b72-5c63-aec6-2e477be64e8b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{x}{2\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315582} {:answer/id #uuid "764ddf91-8316-5d5f-ae60-fa33202df071" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{x}{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315785} {:answer/id #uuid "82fa34d1-254e-5284-8f46-340093da20bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{x}{4\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315841}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315582} :answer-field/id #uuid "f855de14-5251-533c-9172-a67d92d9e753" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317132}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "f7afbc4a-cfc0-549a-b6ce-77b93f2927bc" :question/math-academy-id "q-343907" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation ${y}^{″}+{\\pi }^{2}y=f(x),$ where the $4$-periodic forcing function is given by \n\n$$\nf(x)∼2+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{4}{{n}^{2}}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}).\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options in the reasoning below regarding the resonant component of a particular solution of the equation.\n\nThe natural frequency of the system matches the frequency of the {{field-1}} harmonic of the forcing function. \n\nConsequently, the particular solution contains a resonant term of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{{p}_{2}}={{field-2}}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We begin by identifying resonance. The differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+{\\pi }^{2}y=f(x)\n$$\n\nhas a natural frequency of \n\n$$\n\\omega =\\sqrt{{\\pi }^{2}}=\\pi .\n$$\n\nThe frequencies of the forcing harmonics for a $4$-periodic function $(L=2)$ are \n\n$$\n{\\omega }_{n}=\\frac{n\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nFor $n=2$, we have ${\\omega }_{2}=\\pi .$ Since ${\\omega }_{2}=\\omega ,$ the $n=2$ harmonic of the forcing function causes resonance.\n\nFor the $n=2$ harmonic, the forcing term is \n\n$$\n{f}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x).\n$$\n\nBecause this frequency matches the natural frequency, we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{{p}_{2}}=x(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)).\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{{p}_{2}}^{′} & =(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x))+x(-\\pi A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)+\\pi B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x)) \\\\ {y}_{{p}_{2}}^{″} & =-2\\pi A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)+2\\pi B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x)-{\\pi }^{2}x(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the expressions for ${y}_{{p}_{2}}^{″}$ and ${y}_{{p}_{2}}$ into the equation ${y}^{″}+{\\pi }^{2}y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x):$\n\n$$\n[-2\\pi A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)+2\\pi B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x)-{\\pi }^{2}{y}_{{p}_{2}}]+{\\pi }^{2}{y}_{{p}_{2}}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)\n$$\n\nThe resonant terms involving $x$ cancel out, leaving\n\n$$\n-2\\pi A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)+2\\pi B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x).\n$$\n\nNow, equating the sine and cosine coefficients, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-2\\pi A &=1 \\\\ 2\\pi B &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}A &=-\\frac{1}{2\\pi } \\\\ B &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the resonant component of a particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{{p}_{2}}=-\\frac{x}{2\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x).\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "3730dcd9-f2ec-50da-be0d-14c755f56a78"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484349 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484247 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01560cae-7dae-59d2-abe1-84a1d7d6c454" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{27(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}(324-{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2})}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483571} {:answer/id #uuid "4acd6ba8-bd4d-5211-b24b-b92da18d82bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}(324-{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2})}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483701} {:answer/id #uuid "88c5fee9-6d57-52b7-8a9a-fc2fb915b829" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{27(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}(324-{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2})}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483848} {:answer/id #uuid "c8a5f1d7-c219-529e-8a3d-b15c3dd5908b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{27(-1)^{n}}{n(324-{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2})}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483962}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483571} :answer-field/id #uuid "1af9059e-73b7-5797-a59d-c6b481acd050" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484095}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "88bf7711-bb0b-51a9-9d4e-44874b161912" :question/math-academy-id "q-343878" :question/problem "Select the missing parts in a particular solution of the differential equation ${y}^{″}+36y=f(x),$ where the Fourier series of the $6$-periodic forcing function is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{3(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}).\n$$\n\n*Hint: You may assume that the free term and all the sine coefficients in the Fourier series of the particular solution are equal to zero.*\n\nThe Fourier series of a particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,{{field-1}}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We assume that a solution of our equation has the following Fourier series:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}).\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & ∼\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}))∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-{a}_{n}\\frac{n\\pi }{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}) \\\\ {y}_{p}^{″} & ∼\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-{a}_{n}\\frac{n\\pi }{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}))∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-{a}_{n}\\frac{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}{9}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the expressions for ${y}_{p}^{″}$ and ${y}_{p}$ into the equation and have\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-{a}_{n}\\frac{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}{9}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})+36({\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}))={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{3(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}).\n$$\n\nRearranging the terms to find a common denominator:\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}(36-\\frac{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}{9})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{3(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})\n$$\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}(\\frac{324-{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}{9})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{3(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}).\n$$\n\nNow, equating the cosine coefficients:\n\n$$\n\\frac{{a}_{n}(324-{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2})}{9}=\\frac{3(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}}\\,⇒\\,{a}_{n}=\\frac{27(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}(324-{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2})}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,\\frac{27(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}(324-{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2})}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}).\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "c1f72d93-f5f8-5ed4-a7c5-2788d86bcace" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484356}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484354 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186397690 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "26abab5a-e0d3-5a63-b7b9-8c8b067c042b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "n=4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397692} {:answer/id #uuid "1bc27d3d-2228-5745-9f75-6ad665b3203a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "n=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397693} {:answer/id #uuid "c26489de-65c7-593c-9bc1-121a32b98a82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "n=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397694} {:answer/id #uuid "efa4633c-d9b6-5177-bd10-dd0e9bfa3eb3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "n=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397695}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186397695} :answer-field/id #uuid "9d3c5e72-c342-555b-a87c-e14e208988df" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397691} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bc221971-12de-52c7-8478-0068ddd7a170" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{x}{54\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6\\pi x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397697} {:answer/id #uuid "fe05ab27-ffe9-519c-a245-1e3105109d19" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{x}{108\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\pi x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397698} {:answer/id #uuid "96ad6016-80ff-5081-b817-514fd1499c89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{x}{108\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6\\pi x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397699} {:answer/id #uuid "0e910e33-e3b2-5731-a6d5-5a16e1e9ad14" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{x}{216\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6\\pi x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397700}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186397699} :answer-field/id #uuid "9fa9bba7-0c1d-559d-9dd6-0cd8ed6cec73" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397696}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "d694cb78-fd17-5d72-bd5e-fb91875a20a6" :question/math-academy-id "q-343891" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation ${y}^{″}+36{\\pi }^{2}y=f(x),$ where the $1$-periodic forcing function is given by \n\n$$\nf(x)∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{n}^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2n\\pi x).\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options in the reasoning below regarding the resonant component of a particular solution of the equation.\n\nThe natural frequency of the system matches the frequency of the {{field-1}} harmonic of the forcing function. \n\nConsequently, the particular solution contains a resonant term of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{{p}_{3}}={{field-2}}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We begin by identifying resonance. The differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+36{\\pi }^{2}y=f(x)\n$$\n\nhas a natural frequency of \n\n$$\n\\omega =\\sqrt{36{\\pi }^{2}}=6\\pi .\n$$\n\nThe frequencies of the forcing harmonics for a $1$-periodic function $(L=\\frac{1}{2})$ are \n\n$$\n{\\omega }_{n}=\\frac{n\\pi }{(\\frac{1}{2})}=2n\\pi .\n$$\n\nFor $n=3$, we have ${\\omega }_{3}=6\\pi .$ Since ${\\omega }_{3}=\\omega ,$ the $n=3$ harmonic of the forcing function causes resonance.\n\nFor the $n=3$ harmonic, the forcing term is \n\n$$\n{f}_{3}(x)=\\frac{1}{9}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\pi x).\n$$\n\nBecause this frequency matches the natural frequency, we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{{p}_{3}}=x(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\pi x)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6\\pi x)).\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{{p}_{3}}^{′} & =(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\pi x)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6\\pi x))+x(-6\\pi A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6\\pi x)+6\\pi B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\pi x)) \\\\ {y}_{{p}_{3}}^{″} & =-12\\pi A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6\\pi x)+12\\pi B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\pi x)-36{\\pi }^{2}x(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\pi x)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6\\pi x))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the expressions for ${y}_{{p}_{3}}^{″}$ and ${y}_{{p}_{3}}$ into the equation ${y}^{″}+36{\\pi }^{2}y=\\frac{1}{9}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\pi x):$\n\n$$\n[-12\\pi A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6\\pi x)+12\\pi B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\pi x)-36{\\pi }^{2}{y}_{{p}_{3}}]+36{\\pi }^{2}{y}_{{p}_{3}}=\\frac{1}{9}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\pi x)\n$$\n\nThe resonant terms involving $x$ cancel out, leaving\n\n$$\n-12\\pi A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6\\pi x)+12\\pi B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\pi x)=\\frac{1}{9}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\pi x).\n$$\n\nNow, equating the sine and cosine coefficients, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-12\\pi A &=0 \\\\ 12\\pi B &=\\frac{1}{9}\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}A &=0 \\\\ B &=\\frac{1}{108\\pi }\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the resonant component of a particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{{p}_{3}}=\\frac{x}{108\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6\\pi x).\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "d16e1bd2-5d3a-57fc-8ce5-5535cc365650" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484314}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484356 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317450 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "474ff160-2d86-5c69-8f85-60a047da5ab3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+8)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315534} {:answer/id #uuid "708fd803-efd9-508d-a532-3adcfc75816c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{3}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+8)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315751} {:answer/id #uuid "c6c52c8f-3852-5f92-80be-6b94f3557303" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{3}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+8)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316242} {:answer/id #uuid "ec8a1480-b239-5433-afdd-61831369aa0f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{3(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{3}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+8)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316430}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315751} :answer-field/id #uuid "338e5d77-3b2a-5f0d-9914-332ea1ae1ee2" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316666} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02955be2-58f0-51d6-9945-0544a0ee1d0f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315130} {:answer/id #uuid "04fb5a85-7568-58b9-89ef-e9f8c617bb00" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315142} {:answer/id #uuid "0af17db2-155c-57c1-915c-b2b4ebbb8456" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315177} {:answer/id #uuid "c6644cb0-f07f-5b82-9b35-30e6d381997b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316237}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186316237} :answer-field/id #uuid "7fcafd77-7898-5bd9-bcc7-456c9841599f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316850}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "6d225572-fd74-5350-9e69-efe599d6dfa7" :question/math-academy-id "q-343883" :question/problem "Select the missing parts in a particular solution of the differential equation ${y}^{″}-8y=f(x),$ where the Fourier series of the $2$-periodic forcing function is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)∼8+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{3(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{3}\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x).\n$$\n\n*Hint: You may assume that all the cosine coefficients in the Fourier series of the particular solution are equal to zero.*\n\nThe Fourier series of a particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}∼{{field-1}}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,{{field-2}}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x)\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We assume that a solution of our equation has the following Fourier series:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x).\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & ∼\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x))∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}n\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x) \\\\ {y}_{p}^{″} & ∼\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}n\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x))∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-{b}_{n}{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the expressions for ${y}_{p}^{″}$ and ${y}_{p}$ into the equation and have\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-{b}_{n}{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x)-8(\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x))=8+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{3(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{3}\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x).\n$$\n\nRearranging the terms, we get \n\n$$\n-8\\cdot \\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}(-{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}-8)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x)=8+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{3(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{3}\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x).\n$$\n\nNow, equating the free terms and sine coefficients, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-8\\cdot \\frac{{a}_{0}}{2} &=8 \\\\ {b}_{n}(-{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}-8) &=\\frac{3(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{3}\\pi }\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2} &=-1 \\\\ {b}_{n} &=\\frac{3(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{3}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+8)}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}∼-1+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,\\frac{3(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{3}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+8)}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x).\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "e1c91699-1134-58dc-ab5b-ea077a7ce0b0" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484354}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484357 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484267 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2cf7e913-fe58-5754-9149-b09245f9b60a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{16}{{n}^{2}({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+64)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483642} {:answer/id #uuid "53e18eaf-8bd1-5628-92d7-801bfaf1946a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{16}{{n}^{2}({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+64)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483717} {:answer/id #uuid "6175ce1f-2050-5a57-bf7b-da70f7498dcd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{4}{{n}^{2}({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+64)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483754} {:answer/id #uuid "b701f81d-8ac2-5b72-b41d-29bb579466f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{16}{n({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+64)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483935}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483642} :answer-field/id #uuid "fe77b4de-90c9-5d84-bcb6-651adb4b6345" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484192}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "b49a66f8-383d-5ed3-a1a9-b2f05fd8c09d" :question/math-academy-id "q-343873" :question/problem "Select the missing parts in a particular solution of the differential equation ${y}^{″}-16y=f(x),$ where the Fourier series of the $4$-periodic forcing function is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{4}{{n}^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}).\n$$\n\n*Hint: You may assume that the free term and all the sine coefficients in the Fourier series of the particular solution are equal to zero.*\n\nThe Fourier series of a particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,{{field-1}}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2})\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We assume that a solution of our equation has the following Fourier series:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}).\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & ∼\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}))∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-{a}_{n}\\frac{n\\pi }{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}) \\\\ {y}_{p}^{″} & ∼\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-{a}_{n}\\frac{n\\pi }{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}))∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-{a}_{n}\\frac{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the expressions for ${y}_{p}^{″}$ and ${y}_{p}$ into the equation and have\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-{a}_{n}\\frac{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2})-16({\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}))={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{4}{{n}^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}).\n$$\n\nRearranging the terms, we get \n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-{a}_{n}(\\frac{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}{4}+16)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2})={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{4}{{n}^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}).\n$$\n\nNow, equating the cosine coefficients, we obtain \n\n$$\n-{a}_{n}(\\frac{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}{4}+16)=\\frac{4}{{n}^{2}}\\,⇒\\,{a}_{n}=-\\frac{16}{{n}^{2}({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+64)}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,-\\frac{16}{{n}^{2}({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+64)}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}).\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "e69e4c87-91c4-5dd6-a2e7-92fb618add77" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484349}}] :activity/title "Solving ODEs Using Fourier Series" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483556}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046644} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507577} :activity/id #uuid "efb16358-d54d-56ff-8468-25f5c93d3c7b" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049288 :topic/id #uuid "ea0735e9-7712-5a1c-a626-25cab33f54cb" :topic/math-academy-id 6386} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507537 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368362 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "122300c0-651a-5232-86c6-033d51da03b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{8x}{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368364} {:answer/id #uuid "dd80f01e-6d8e-536a-823c-7056a3b8e1a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{2x}{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368365} {:answer/id #uuid "1d2cdeac-9e0a-5c78-8be5-826656897a3a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{4x}{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368366} {:answer/id #uuid "94f19c63-b8e4-5cc8-bbcf-7867a07f6258" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4x}{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368367}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368366} :answer-field/id #uuid "45d93abd-3293-5cd5-b7bf-40e46fb373a3" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368363} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "43c19f30-8cb0-5c94-bc48-e088f92bd54d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "n=4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368369} {:answer/id #uuid "505b3f74-4f8e-58d3-bd80-0663f572435a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "n=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368370} {:answer/id #uuid "4c4b72a7-1c1f-532e-95af-dd53910e34d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "n=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368371} {:answer/id #uuid "3ff458e0-f735-5d03-a067-564058720262" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "n=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368372}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368370} :answer-field/id #uuid "5da0e00c-bb8a-5ae4-9075-989b21ab79e4" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368368}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "c5a48baf-9ceb-513a-885b-da45d3ee0d03" :question/math-academy-id "q-343911" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation ${y}^{″}+\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}y=f(x),$ where the $4$-periodic forcing function is given by \n\n$$\nf(x)∼-2+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{4}{{n}^{2}}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}).\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options in the reasoning below regarding the resonant component of a particular solution of the equation.\n\nThe natural frequency of the system matches the frequency of the {{field-1}} harmonic of the forcing function. \n\nConsequently, the particular solution contains a resonant term of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{{p}_{1}}={{field-2}}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We begin by identifying resonance. The differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}y=f(x)\n$$\n\nhas a natural frequency of \n\n$$\n\\omega =\\sqrt{\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}}=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nThe frequencies of the forcing harmonics for a $4$-periodic function $(L=2)$ are \n\n$$\n{\\omega }_{n}=\\frac{n\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nFor $n=1$, we have ${\\omega }_{1}=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ Since ${\\omega }_{1}=\\omega ,$ the $n=1$ harmonic of the forcing function causes resonance.\n\nFor the $n=1$ harmonic, the forcing term is \n\n$$\n{f}_{1}(x)=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2}).\n$$\n\nBecause this frequency matches the natural frequency, we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{{p}_{1}}=x(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2})+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2})).\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{{p}_{1}}^{′} & =(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2})+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2}))+x(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2})+\\frac{\\pi }{2}B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2})) \\\\ {y}_{{p}_{1}}^{″} & =-\\pi A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2})+\\pi B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2})-\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}x(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2})+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2}))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the expressions for ${y}_{{p}_{1}}^{″}$ and ${y}_{{p}_{1}}$ into the equation ${y}^{″}+\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2}):$\n\n$$\n[-\\pi A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2})+\\pi B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2})-\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}{y}_{{p}_{1}}]+\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}{y}_{{p}_{1}}=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2})\n$$\n\nThe resonant terms involving $x$ cancel out, leaving\n\n$$\n-\\pi A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2})+\\pi B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2})=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2}).\n$$\n\nNow, equating the sine and cosine coefficients, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-\\pi A &=4 \\\\ \\pi B &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}A &=-\\frac{4}{\\pi } \\\\ B &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the resonant component of a particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{{p}_{1}}=-\\frac{4x}{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2}).\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "1e364e0b-e003-503e-aadb-895d96ad9926" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507557}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507540 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507450 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d7f8754-41a4-514c-86d1-d5647b54333b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{98\\pi }{{n}^{2}(49-{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2})}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506703} {:answer/id #uuid "a564bbb9-af52-596a-bcf1-bb101438423e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{2\\pi }{{n}^{2}(49-{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2})}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507039} {:answer/id #uuid "d3e53c4f-ca05-5e48-9e4b-fba6af34790c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\pi }{{n}^{2}(49-{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2})}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507136} {:answer/id #uuid "e6ebfba2-6c04-5678-a8f3-079e02b2c158" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\pi }{n(49-{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2})}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507185}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507136} :answer-field/id #uuid "f8a63a77-7e75-5948-b4a4-f7660df42d29" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507393}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "776f8284-bdba-5ab6-8f5c-16f7e1be5e32" :question/math-academy-id "q-343877" :question/problem "Select the missing parts in a particular solution of the differential equation ${y}^{″}+49y=f(x),$ where the Fourier series of the $2$-periodic forcing function is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2\\pi }{{n}^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x).\n$$\n\n*Hint: You may assume that the free term and all the sine coefficients in the Fourier series of the particular solution are equal to zero.*\n\nThe Fourier series of a particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,{{field-1}}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x)\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We assume that a solution of our equation has the following Fourier series:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x).\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & ∼\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x))∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-{a}_{n}n\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x) \\\\ {y}_{p}^{″} & ∼\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-{a}_{n}n\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x))∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-{a}_{n}{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the expressions for ${y}_{p}^{″}$ and ${y}_{p}$ into the equation and have\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-{a}_{n}{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x)+49({\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x))={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2\\pi }{{n}^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x).\n$$\n\nRearranging the terms, we get \n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}(49-{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x)={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2\\pi }{{n}^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x).\n$$\n\nNow, equating the cosine coefficients, we obtain \n\n$$\n{a}_{n}(49-{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2})=\\frac{2\\pi }{{n}^{2}}\\,⇒\\,{a}_{n}=\\frac{2\\pi }{{n}^{2}(49-{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2})}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,\\frac{2\\pi }{{n}^{2}(49-{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2})}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x).\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "29506e54-5d8b-54a1-b9a6-9e847bc7683d" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507552}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507552 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507448 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "15b056ad-c695-5840-bd81-265a5a05fdaa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{x}{54\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\pi x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506720} {:answer/id #uuid "2ae162dd-9009-571c-8a32-a223b4b316b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{x}{108\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\pi x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506779} {:answer/id #uuid "40e382b8-4b9d-57ca-b2bd-cc8b191c72b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{x}{27\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\pi x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506828} {:answer/id #uuid "7e847ab9-d2f2-5bd1-a314-8b4ab67840a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{x}{54\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\pi x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506956}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506956} :answer-field/id #uuid "ab909fc9-81ec-5132-a654-5b476b6505de" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507348} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "110da6e6-f9ed-5990-8e21-185d0c9edf82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "n=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506712} {:answer/id #uuid "22cce65c-606a-579a-a1d5-eed3f3596381" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "n=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506751} {:answer/id #uuid "cbdbd401-7edc-5f4d-9c1c-340d4823c668" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "n=4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507122} {:answer/id #uuid "edc46441-d5cb-51fb-9094-79f71f9202c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "n=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507203}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506712} :answer-field/id #uuid "ce9a6688-96f8-5a1f-9a15-b61299b1c973" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507367}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "735d1714-8711-57ac-8156-7342afc0beb4" :question/math-academy-id "q-343900" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation ${y}^{″}+9{\\pi }^{2}y=f(x),$ where the $2$-periodic forcing function is given by \n\n$$\nf(x)∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{n}^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x).\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options in the reasoning below regarding the resonant component of a particular solution of the equation.\n\nThe natural frequency of the system matches the frequency of the {{field-1}} harmonic of the forcing function. \n\nConsequently, the particular solution contains a resonant term of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{{p}_{3}}={{field-2}}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We begin by identifying resonance. The differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+9{\\pi }^{2}y=f(x)\n$$\n\nhas a natural frequency of \n\n$$\n\\omega =\\sqrt{9{\\pi }^{2}}=3\\pi .\n$$\n\nThe frequencies of the forcing harmonics for a $2$-periodic function $(L=1)$ are \n\n$$\n{\\omega }_{n}=\\frac{n\\pi }{1}=n\\pi .\n$$\n\nFor $n=3$, we have ${\\omega }_{3}=3\\pi .$ Since ${\\omega }_{3}=\\omega ,$ the $n=3$ harmonic of the forcing function causes resonance.\n\nFor the $n=3$ harmonic, the forcing term is \n\n$$\n{f}_{3}(x)=\\frac{1}{9}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\pi x).\n$$\n\nBecause this frequency matches the natural frequency, we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{{p}_{3}}=x(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\pi x)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\pi x)).\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{{p}_{3}}^{′} & =(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\pi x)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\pi x))+x(-3\\pi A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\pi x)+3\\pi B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\pi x)) \\\\ {y}_{{p}_{3}}^{″} & =-6\\pi A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\pi x)+6\\pi B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\pi x)-9{\\pi }^{2}x(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\pi x)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\pi x))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the expressions for ${y}_{{p}_{3}}^{″}$ and ${y}_{{p}_{3}}$ into the equation ${y}^{″}+9{\\pi }^{2}y=\\frac{1}{9}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\pi x):$\n\n$$\n[-6\\pi A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\pi x)+6\\pi B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\pi x)-9{\\pi }^{2}{y}_{{p}_{3}}]+9{\\pi }^{2}{y}_{{p}_{3}}=\\frac{1}{9}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\pi x)\n$$\n\nThe resonant terms involving $x$ cancel out, leaving\n\n$$\n-6\\pi A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\pi x)+6\\pi B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\pi x)=\\frac{1}{9}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\pi x).\n$$\n\nNow, equating the sine and cosine coefficients, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-6\\pi A &=0 \\\\ 6\\pi B &=\\frac{1}{9}\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}A &=0 \\\\ B &=\\frac{1}{54\\pi }\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the resonant component of a particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{{p}_{3}}=\\frac{x}{54\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\pi x).\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "58ba9283-6ba3-5bb3-8a8e-3db7151c4592" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507537}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507557 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507508 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4163fb4e-1673-56b1-9e81-b3f9b76bdcdb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "n=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506831} {:answer/id #uuid "b513ed2d-2ca6-556b-9cdc-932a781ed446" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "n=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507077} {:answer/id #uuid "bdc323db-b9be-5ba3-89bf-74f4864af6f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "n=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507097} {:answer/id #uuid "ef817cb8-b7c8-5338-9ccb-b25eb7ba3fdb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "n=4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507207}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507077} :answer-field/id #uuid "955e89a0-7f01-5af1-93d8-b8760f8a0f25" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507334} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "29bab42e-19fd-5999-bca5-a7f709334255" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{9x}{16\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506773} {:answer/id #uuid "7380ffe2-b480-549e-b9ff-55f23f096330" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{9x}{8\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506943} {:answer/id #uuid "c1abe79a-867b-5b61-b180-db62e3c89468" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{9x}{32\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507105} {:answer/id #uuid "db644471-744d-5974-86ca-8c2d68c380d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{9x}{16\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507154}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506773} :answer-field/id #uuid "e28d2851-cb92-5497-aeb3-e6113495aa58" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507377}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "d683633f-d60f-57e1-b38c-1931a877a7fb" :question/math-academy-id "q-343914" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation ${y}^{″}+\\frac{4{\\pi }^{2}}{9}y=f(x),$ where the $6$-periodic forcing function is given by \n\n$$\nf(x)∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{3(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{2}}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}).\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options in the reasoning below regarding the resonant component of a particular solution of the equation.\n\nThe natural frequency of the system matches the frequency of the {{field-1}} harmonic of the forcing function. \n\nConsequently, the particular solution contains a resonant term of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{{p}_{2}}={{field-2}}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We begin by identifying resonance. The differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+\\frac{4{\\pi }^{2}}{9}y=f(x)\n$$\n\nhas a natural frequency of \n\n$$\n\\omega =\\sqrt{\\frac{4{\\pi }^{2}}{9}}=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.\n$$\n\nThe frequencies of the forcing harmonics for a $6$-periodic function $(L=3)$ are \n\n$$\n{\\omega }_{n}=\\frac{n\\pi }{3}.\n$$\n\nFor $n=2$, we have ${\\omega }_{2}=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.$ Since ${\\omega }_{2}=\\omega ,$ the $n=2$ harmonic of the forcing function causes resonance.\n\nFor the $n=2$ harmonic, the forcing term is \n\n$$\n{f}_{2}(x)=-\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3}).\n$$\n\nBecause this frequency matches the natural frequency, we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{{p}_{2}}=x(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3})+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3})).\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{y}_{{p}_{2}}^{′} & =(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3})+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3}))+x(-\\frac{2\\pi }{3}A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3})+\\frac{2\\pi }{3}B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3})) \\\\ \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{y}_{{p}_{2}}^{″} & =-\\frac{4\\pi }{3}A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3})+\\frac{4\\pi }{3}B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3})-\\frac{4{\\pi }^{2}}{9}x(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3})+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3}))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the expressions for ${y}_{{p}_{2}}^{″}$ and ${y}_{{p}_{2}}$ into the equation ${y}^{″}+\\frac{4{\\pi }^{2}}{9}y=-\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3}):$\n\n$$\n\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,[-\\frac{4\\pi }{3}A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3})+\\frac{4\\pi }{3}B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3})-\\frac{4{\\pi }^{2}}{9}{y}_{{p}_{2}}]+\\frac{4{\\pi }^{2}}{9}{y}_{{p}_{2}}=-\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3})\n$$\n\nThe resonant terms involving $x$ cancel out, leaving\n\n$$\n-\\frac{4\\pi }{3}A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3})+\\frac{4\\pi }{3}B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3})=-\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3}).\n$$\n\nNow, equating the sine and cosine coefficients, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{4\\pi }{3}A &=-\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ \\frac{4\\pi }{3}B &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}A &=\\frac{9}{16\\pi } \\\\ B &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the resonant component of a particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{{p}_{2}}=\\frac{9x}{16\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3}).\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "7136d128-593a-539d-a5b4-9cc5e8e074f4"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507577 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507482 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07607d87-94c7-5615-8410-d21177e24ce4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{{n}^{2}({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+4)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506689} {:answer/id #uuid "6957dcc3-1d7f-5d45-8049-c8f637d3dde2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{n({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+4)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506924} {:answer/id #uuid "9ff40b82-1458-59f6-ab1d-32e43630183c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{{n}^{2}({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+4)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507021} {:answer/id #uuid "ccb3a7af-4a78-5819-9be4-1fc493c69627" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507124}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506689} :answer-field/id #uuid "09882a98-7e0e-5272-9868-612a26c52a00" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507245}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "af83bf7a-2326-5550-86bc-d86a83bd79bc" :question/math-academy-id "q-343874" :question/problem "Select the missing parts in a particular solution of the differential equation ${y}^{″}-4y=f(x),$ where the Fourier series of the $2$-periodic forcing function is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{{n}^{2}}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x).\n$$\n\n*Hint: You may assume that the free term and all the cosine coefficients in the Fourier series of the particular solution are equal to zero.*\n\nThe Fourier series of a particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,{{field-1}}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x)\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We assume that a solution of our equation has the following Fourier series:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x).\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & ∼\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x))∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}n\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x) \\\\ {y}_{p}^{″} & ∼\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}n\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x))∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-{b}_{n}{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the expressions for ${y}_{p}^{″}$ and ${y}_{p}$ into the equation and have\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-{b}_{n}{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x)-4({\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x))={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{{n}^{2}}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x).\n$$\n\nRearranging the terms, we get \n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-{b}_{n}({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+4)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x)={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{{n}^{2}}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x).\n$$\n\nNow, equating the sine coefficients, we obtain \n\n$$\n-{b}_{n}({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+4)=\\frac{2}{{n}^{2}}\\,⇒\\,{b}_{n}=-\\frac{2}{{n}^{2}({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+4)}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,-\\frac{2}{{n}^{2}({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+4)}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x).\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "d3262fec-399e-5719-a5fb-ab0190064e6f" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507540}}] :activity/title "Solving ODEs Using Fourier Series" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506675}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046644} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512976} :activity/id #uuid "5d2d2b52-5ff3-5595-845a-94f1c9e2f4a2" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049288 :topic/id #uuid "ea0735e9-7712-5a1c-a626-25cab33f54cb" :topic/math-academy-id 6386} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512976 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512900 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0da3558a-014b-530b-8b42-aea977dc4e14" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512125} {:answer/id #uuid "62559ed5-80cf-5704-bf90-5935af3f40e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512332} {:answer/id #uuid "9009e0f7-5d50-5aa5-a503-ea03f3e99675" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512439} {:answer/id #uuid "914f8df8-af61-5f9e-a17f-75583f136c10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512444}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512439} :answer-field/id #uuid "58617cc3-c088-55aa-805f-3f4f74b88173" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512774} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "20e270f2-1405-5a3a-bdf2-62a39dd31496" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}{\\pi }^{2}}{\\pi +{n}^{3}{\\pi }^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512168} {:answer/id #uuid "283b9537-1996-5ae6-a737-f53e608ac594" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}(1-{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2})}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512189} {:answer/id #uuid "e84259ba-5de4-5201-9e89-fe85cb2ea4c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}}{n\\pi (1+{n}^{2}\\pi )}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512654} {:answer/id #uuid "e9dd1e71-1b42-5658-b30c-6f8df61b1ce8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{3}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+1)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512661}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512661} :answer-field/id #uuid "b53af9ba-07ea-5f68-ae7e-da94231dbd26" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512822}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "7658d4f4-3b1c-50cc-bb09-9642192fb8ae" :question/math-academy-id "q-342372" :question/problem "Select the missing parts in a particular solution of the differential equation ${y}^{″}-y=f(x),$ where the Fourier series of the $2$-periodic forcing function is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)∼1+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{3}\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x).\n$$\n\n*Hint: You may assume that all the sine coefficients in the Fourier series of the particular solution are equal to zero.*\n\nThe Fourier series of a particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}∼{{field-1}}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,{{field-2}}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x)\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We assume that a solution of our equation has the following Fourier series:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x).\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & ∼\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x))∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-{a}_{n}n\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x) \\\\ {y}_{p}^{″} & ∼\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-{a}_{n}n\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x))∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-{a}_{n}{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the expressions for ${y}_{p}^{″}$ and ${y}_{p}$ into the equation and have\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-{a}_{n}{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x)-(\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x))=1+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{3}\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x).\n$$\n\nRearranging the terms, we get \n\n$$\n-\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}(-{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}-1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x)=1+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{3}\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x).\n$$\n\nNow, equating the free terms and cosine coefficients, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2} &=1 \\\\ {a}_{n}(-{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}-1) &=\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{3}\\pi }\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2} &=-1 \\\\ {a}_{n} &=\\frac{(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{3}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+1)}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}∼-1+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,\\frac{(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{3}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+1)}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x).\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "38154cfd-c70d-53d6-a6c1-72899f8c40bb" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186513019}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512998 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317451 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0775efd4-349e-5347-a0ab-209c8617865e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{6x}{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315155} {:answer/id #uuid "3c6860ca-41e8-5f7c-9922-b06de92ebf64" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{6x}{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315465} {:answer/id #uuid "a683ea58-3eb4-513d-ae1d-5c5ee8f548e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{12x}{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316056} {:answer/id #uuid "f37afc9b-a1f3-5052-8c94-74b4bd652d0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3x}{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316477}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315465} :answer-field/id #uuid "69639b9f-3bb7-58ec-a83d-c19f4137e2fc" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316800} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c8e5254-d482-5016-83a5-2d062da9f632" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "n=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315193} {:answer/id #uuid "464643b7-419c-5207-ac7e-9b750b301b15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "n=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315524} {:answer/id #uuid "85cff5d8-8660-5f09-837d-b9a7c81dfdb3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "n=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315864} {:answer/id #uuid "f2f486c4-6a97-5a85-bb1d-c46b9ac5c8b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "n=4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316471}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315864} :answer-field/id #uuid "ec2fdc4d-025d-5256-ba3f-588326c84d79" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317105}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "a67d7600-1f79-59aa-9051-5cda440b9948" :question/math-academy-id "q-343901" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation ${y}^{″}+\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}y=f(x),$ where the $4$-periodic forcing function is given by \n\n$$\nf(x)∼-2+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{6}{{n}^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}).\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options in the reasoning below regarding the resonant component of a particular solution of the equation.\n\nThe natural frequency of the system matches the frequency of the {{field-1}} harmonic of the forcing function. \n\nConsequently, the particular solution contains a resonant term of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{{p}_{1}}={{field-2}}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We begin by identifying resonance. The differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}y=f(x)\n$$\n\nhas a natural frequency of \n\n$$\n\\omega =\\sqrt{\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}}=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nThe frequencies of the forcing harmonics for a $4$-periodic function $(L=2)$ are \n\n$$\n{\\omega }_{n}=\\frac{n\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nFor $n=1$, we have ${\\omega }_{1}=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ Since ${\\omega }_{1}=\\omega ,$ the $n=1$ harmonic of the forcing function causes resonance.\n\nFor the $n=1$ harmonic, the forcing term is \n\n$$\n{f}_{1}(x)=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2}).\n$$\n\nBecause this frequency matches the natural frequency, we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{{p}_{1}}=x(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2})+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2})).\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{{p}_{1}}^{′} & =(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2})+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2}))+x(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2})+\\frac{\\pi }{2}B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2})) \\\\ {y}_{{p}_{1}}^{″} & =-\\pi A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2})+\\pi B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2})-\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}x(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2})+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2}))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the expressions for ${y}_{{p}_{1}}^{″}$ and ${y}_{{p}_{1}}$ into the equation ${y}^{″}+\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}y=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2}):$\n\n$$\n[-\\pi A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2})+\\pi B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2})-\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}{y}_{{p}_{1}}]+\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}{y}_{{p}_{1}}=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2})\n$$\n\nThe resonant terms involving $x$ cancel out, leaving\n\n$$\n-\\pi A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2})+\\pi B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2})=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2}).\n$$\n\nNow, equating the sine and cosine coefficients, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-\\pi A &=0 \\\\ \\pi B &=6\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}A &=0 \\\\ B &=\\frac{6}{\\pi }\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the resonant component of a particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{{p}_{1}}=\\frac{6x}{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2}).\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "7402cf38-a638-59a3-b9f9-c0865bae0160"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186513000 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368351 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6f942acd-fc3f-553e-9236-b60c2312cab0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{3x}{2\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368353} {:answer/id #uuid "ee5c562e-9d36-5f0d-8f3c-9b02f345cfa2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3x}{4\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368354} {:answer/id #uuid "e756ccee-191e-5f8c-ad6f-5baa620bb61c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3x}{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368355} {:answer/id #uuid "0e8af9a5-eef4-597d-8a2e-bb0dd59701c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{6x}{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368356}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368355} :answer-field/id #uuid "df91d777-147f-5107-937e-39046d30908d" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368352} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e0d30efb-5e3f-5069-bdd1-edd81b1cb1ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "n=4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368358} {:answer/id #uuid "3fe8b930-35c5-54c4-94ff-65189b49b3c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "n=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368359} {:answer/id #uuid "f4a07916-0005-53d4-80ac-6d722dff7217" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "n=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368360} {:answer/id #uuid "eb23d851-2d00-5cda-9fc1-a2a5b81ab7ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "n=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368361}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368359} :answer-field/id #uuid "3e418679-4b7e-51b6-a85a-3056974bca2f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368357}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "5d1812d8-39e4-5dd4-b731-c6eb976d76d3" :question/math-academy-id "q-343896" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation ${y}^{″}+\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{9}y=f(x),$ where the $6$-periodic forcing function is given by \n\n$$\nf(x)∼2+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{n}^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}).\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options in the reasoning below regarding the resonant component of a particular solution of the equation.\n\nThe natural frequency of the system matches the frequency of the {{field-1}} harmonic of the forcing function. \n\nConsequently, the particular solution contains a resonant term of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{{p}_{1}}={{field-2}}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We begin by identifying resonance. The differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{9}y=f(x)\n$$\n\nhas a natural frequency of \n\n$$\n\\omega =\\sqrt{\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{9}}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.\n$$\n\nThe frequencies of the forcing harmonics for a $6$-periodic function $(L=3)$ are \n\n$$\n{\\omega }_{n}=\\frac{n\\pi }{3}.\n$$\n\nFor $n=1$, we have ${\\omega }_{1}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$ Since ${\\omega }_{1}=\\omega ,$ the $n=1$ harmonic of the forcing function causes resonance.\n\nFor the $n=1$ harmonic, the forcing term is \n\n$$\n{f}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{3}).\n$$\n\nBecause this frequency matches the natural frequency, we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{{p}_{1}}=x(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{3})+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{3})).\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{{p}_{1}}^{′} & =(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{3})+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{3}))+x(-\\frac{\\pi }{3}A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{3})+\\frac{\\pi }{3}B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{3})) \\\\ {y}_{{p}_{1}}^{″} & =-\\frac{2\\pi }{3}A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{3})+\\frac{2\\pi }{3}B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{3})-\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{9}x(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{3})+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{3}))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the expressions for ${y}_{{p}_{1}}^{″}$ and ${y}_{{p}_{1}}$ into the equation ${y}^{″}+\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{9}y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{3}):$\n\n$$\n[-\\frac{2\\pi }{3}A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{3})+\\frac{2\\pi }{3}B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{3})-\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{9}{y}_{{p}_{1}}]+\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{9}{y}_{{p}_{1}}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{3})\n$$\n\nThe resonant terms involving $x$ cancel out, leaving\n\n$$\n-\\frac{2\\pi }{3}A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{3})+\\frac{2\\pi }{3}B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{3})=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{3}).\n$$\n\nNow, equating the sine and cosine coefficients, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2\\pi }{3}A &=0 \\\\ \\frac{2\\pi }{3}B &=1\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}A &=0 \\\\ B &=\\frac{3}{2\\pi }\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the resonant component of a particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{{p}_{1}}=\\frac{3x}{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{3}).\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "8730a614-36f9-5c60-80e1-eac2fd3a8305" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512998}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186513002 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368337 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2077a0e4-25ea-549f-b18b-eeaee168ff0e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368339} {:answer/id #uuid "cf025f55-d758-53ad-ba9a-e6003ea863d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368340} {:answer/id #uuid "4926bd85-afe3-53d9-b42e-08dd5f26bb22" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368341} {:answer/id #uuid "70f39c4d-5178-5b56-a752-489a2b9d7ed4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368342}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368341} :answer-field/id #uuid "099f2aa9-cfdf-59eb-bd13-a3254f3a71f5" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368338} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cb9c3ae4-a918-591f-9eba-4ebc030650cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{3}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+54)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368344} {:answer/id #uuid "b0fe33c1-fafa-55ee-a956-b6576f020afa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{18(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{3}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+54)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368345} {:answer/id #uuid "079feac5-65d5-5c90-8e5d-94615aae162a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{18(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{3}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+54)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368346} {:answer/id #uuid "78348f45-f808-5940-ba48-bfbdb844fa02" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{18(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+54)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368347}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368345} :answer-field/id #uuid "1055c2d2-985c-506c-98b6-2c511358a08c" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368343}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "3769a7a2-440c-52c1-8785-e3df2dc4c9a6" :question/math-academy-id "q-343885" :question/problem "Select the missing parts in a particular solution of the differential equation ${y}^{″}-6y=f(x),$ where the Fourier series of the $6$-periodic forcing function is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)∼6+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{3}\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}).\n$$\n\n*Hint: You may assume that all the cosine coefficients in the Fourier series of the particular solution are equal to zero.*\n\nThe Fourier series of a particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}∼{{field-1}}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,{{field-2}}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We assume that a solution of our equation has the following Fourier series:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}).\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & ∼\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}))∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{b}_{n}n\\pi }{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}) \\\\ {y}_{p}^{″} & ∼\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{b}_{n}n\\pi }{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}))∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-\\frac{{b}_{n}{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}{9}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the expressions for ${y}_{p}^{″}$ and ${y}_{p}$ into the equation and have\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-\\frac{{b}_{n}{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}{9}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})-6(\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}))=6+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{3}\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}).\n$$\n\nRearranging the terms, we get \n\n$$\n-6\\cdot \\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}(-\\frac{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}{9}-6)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})=6+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{3}\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}).\n$$\n\nNow, equating the free terms and sine coefficients, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-6\\cdot \\frac{{a}_{0}}{2} &=6 \\\\ {b}_{n}(-\\frac{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}{9}-6) &=\\frac{2(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{3}\\pi }\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2} &=-1 \\\\ {b}_{n} &=\\frac{18(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{3}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+54)}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}∼-1+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,\\frac{18(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{3}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+54)}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}).\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "9099693d-f185-5899-981c-a69e22a33e27" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186513000}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186513019 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512930 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4cb46238-0858-5e29-93f5-3b1abf6e8453" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{135(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+225)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512264} {:answer/id #uuid "5bc4c62d-f986-5b1e-8753-4ec4676a3b8f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{15(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+225)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512311} {:answer/id #uuid "b6d76ec8-e500-538c-8442-85b23edc88a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{135(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+225)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512535}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512535} :answer-field/id #uuid "4e6a7300-844d-5db4-8005-d85823b210da" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512770} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9336c13e-c8cb-5577-898b-73fc979337d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{45(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+225)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512448} {:answer/id #uuid "9e473653-b9e6-5e7c-a302-54a5b439449d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{9(-1)^{n}}{n({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+225)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512469} {:answer/id #uuid "dadc4114-78f9-5557-a713-9afe7cf481ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{9(-1)^{n}}{n({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+225)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512621}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512469} :answer-field/id #uuid "a5322b59-ac05-57c5-bebd-3ff95532c124" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512813}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "b98de4ab-a079-5d39-b165-ba7998caa236" :question/math-academy-id "q-343834" :question/problem "Select the missing parts in a particular solution of the differential equation ${y}^{′}+5y=f(x),$ where the Fourier series of the $6$-periodic forcing function is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{3(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}\\pi }cos\\,(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}).\n$$\n\nThe Fourier series of a particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,{{field-1}}\\cdot cos\\,(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})+{{field-2}}\\cdot sin\\,(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We assume that a solution of our equation has the following Fourier series:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}cos\\,(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})+{b}_{n}sin\\,(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}).\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & ∼\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}cos\\,(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})+{b}_{n}sin\\,(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})) \\\\  & ∼0+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\cdot (-\\frac{n\\pi }{3}sin\\,(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}))+{b}_{n}\\cdot \\frac{n\\pi }{3}cos\\,(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}) \\\\  & ∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{3}sin\\,(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})+\\frac{{b}_{n}n\\pi }{3}cos\\,(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the expressions for ${y}_{p}^{′}$ and ${y}_{p}$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & ({\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{3}sin\\,(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})+\\frac{{b}_{n}n\\pi }{3}cos\\,(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})) \\\\  & \\,+5(\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}cos\\,(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})+{b}_{n}sin\\,(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})) \\\\  & \\,\\,={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{3(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}\\pi }cos\\,(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRearranging the terms, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\frac{5{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(\\frac{{b}_{n}n\\pi }{3}+5{a}_{n})cos\\,(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}) \\\\  & \\,\\,+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(-\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{3}+5{b}_{n})sin\\,(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}) \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{3(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}\\pi }cos\\,(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, equating the free terms and the cosine and sine coefficients, we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5{a}_{0}}{2} &=0 \\\\ \\frac{{b}_{n}n\\pi }{3}+5{a}_{n} &=\\frac{3(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}\\pi } \\\\ -\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{3}+5{b}_{n} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, ${a}_{0}=0.$ \n\nNext, from the third equation, \n\n$$\n{b}_{n}=\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{15}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation gives\n\n$$\n(\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{15})\\frac{n\\pi }{3}+5{a}_{n}=\\frac{3(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}\\pi }\\,⇒\\,{a}_{n}=\\frac{135(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+225)}\n$$\n\nTherefore, for ${b}_{n}$ with $n\\ge 1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{b}_{n} & =\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{15} \\\\  & =\\frac{n\\pi }{15}\\cdot \\frac{135(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+225)} \\\\  & =\\frac{9(-1)^{n}}{n({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+225)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,\\frac{135(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+225)}\\cdot cos\\,(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})+\\frac{9(-1)^{n}}{n({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+225)}\\cdot sin\\,(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}).\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "d60f6806-c6cb-503b-a2c8-fff99cda0786" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186513002}}] :activity/title "Solving ODEs Using Fourier Series" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512085}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046644} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186525080} :activity/id #uuid "9264b1c1-1f4d-5132-9bcf-b88ec17fcbba" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049288 :topic/id #uuid "ea0735e9-7712-5a1c-a626-25cab33f54cb" :topic/math-academy-id 6386} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186525075 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524966 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "075ff70c-8adb-53e9-8868-dac30ec95b33" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "n=4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524395} {:answer/id #uuid "11f6e6ae-3c6c-50c6-99cc-458271767c9c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "n=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524412} {:answer/id #uuid "a69c5953-f455-53cf-a745-4db7c010554c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "n=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524646} {:answer/id #uuid "dbf1af4e-c02d-5acb-a898-ed1acac8e812" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "n=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524755}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524755} :answer-field/id #uuid "65f9587c-2102-51c3-9018-cf73e669ee77" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524882} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4e2b8556-b100-5f8e-bc9f-9cd87c21cb7e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{x}{27\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi x}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524513} {:answer/id #uuid "72385776-67d2-5118-b12a-b9ffbb9a1976" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{x}{18\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi x}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524565} {:answer/id #uuid "92909ea3-1683-5e8f-b3a3-8b2af56ef7af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{x}{27\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi x}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524612} {:answer/id #uuid "e88ad1ac-4369-5a83-8114-9aecaa890952" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{x}{54\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi x}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524791}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524612} :answer-field/id #uuid "b41532f6-c5f8-55db-9dde-0e3b62dd91b0" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524919}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "1741c2e1-8000-5ded-a8b9-94a357d2c64a" :question/math-academy-id "q-343887" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation ${y}^{″}+\\frac{9{\\pi }^{2}}{4}y=f(x),$ where the $4$-periodic forcing function is given by \n\n$$\nf(x)∼2+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{n}^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}).\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options in the reasoning below regarding the resonant component of a particular solution of the equation.\n\nThe natural frequency of the system matches the frequency of the {{field-1}} harmonic of the forcing function. \n\nConsequently, the particular solution contains a resonant term of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{{p}_{3}}={{field-2}}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We begin by identifying resonance. The differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+\\frac{9{\\pi }^{2}}{4}y=f(x)\n$$\n\nhas a natural frequency of \n\n$$\n\\omega =\\sqrt{\\frac{9{\\pi }^{2}}{4}}=\\frac{3\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nThe frequencies of the forcing harmonics for a $4$-periodic function $(L=2)$ are \n\n$$\n{\\omega }_{n}=\\frac{n\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nFor $n=3$, we have ${\\omega }_{3}=\\frac{3\\pi }{2}.$ Since ${\\omega }_{3}=\\omega ,$ the $n=3$ harmonic of the forcing function causes resonance.\n\nFor the $n=3$ harmonic, the forcing term is \n\n$$\n{f}_{3}(x)=\\frac{1}{9}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi x}{2}).\n$$\n\nBecause this frequency matches the natural frequency, we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{{p}_{3}}=x(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi x}{2})+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi x}{2})).\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{{p}_{3}}^{′} & =(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi x}{2})+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi x}{2}))+x(-\\frac{3\\pi }{2}A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi x}{2})+\\frac{3\\pi }{2}B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi x}{2})) \\\\ {y}_{{p}_{3}}^{″} & =-3\\pi A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi x}{2})+3\\pi B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi x}{2})-\\frac{9{\\pi }^{2}}{4}x(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi x}{2})+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi x}{2}))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the expressions for ${y}_{{p}_{3}}^{″}$ and ${y}_{{p}_{3}}$ into the equation ${y}^{″}+\\frac{9{\\pi }^{2}}{4}y=\\frac{1}{9}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi x}{2}):$\n\n$$\n[-3\\pi A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi x}{2})+3\\pi B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi x}{2})-\\frac{9{\\pi }^{2}}{4}{y}_{{p}_{3}}]+\\frac{9{\\pi }^{2}}{4}{y}_{{p}_{3}}=\\frac{1}{9}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi x}{2})\n$$\n\nThe resonant terms involving $x$ cancel out, leaving\n\n$$\n-3\\pi A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi x}{2})+3\\pi B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi x}{2})=\\frac{1}{9}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi x}{2}).\n$$\n\nNow, equating the sine and cosine coefficients, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-3\\pi A=0 \\\\ 3\\pi B=\\frac{1}{9}\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}A=0 \\\\ B=\\frac{1}{27\\pi }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the resonant component of a particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{{p}_{3}}=\\frac{x}{27\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi x}{2}).\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "195b0b09-5636-555a-9d5e-886ade5b2b0a" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186525085}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186525080 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317447 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "27e72c91-6260-57d4-8f2b-d360cd74d1b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}\\pi (36-{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2})}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315345} {:answer/id #uuid "ca0f4d4a-2783-5d38-996e-52ca4cc7173c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{8(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}\\pi (36-{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2})}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316256} {:answer/id #uuid "e004ee5f-c254-5252-8869-1073aea1cb7c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{8(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}\\pi (36-{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2})}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316361} {:answer/id #uuid "fe8de36c-f73d-5178-bafa-3d6b6a68abaa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{8(-1)^{n}}{n\\pi (36-{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2})}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316543}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186316256} :answer-field/id #uuid "d50cf4cc-94a7-5eeb-8680-cbc2d70101de" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317052}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "3d266502-5760-5926-ac9e-cf2e92540f0b" :question/math-academy-id "q-343875" :question/problem "Select the missing parts in a particular solution of the differential equation ${y}^{″}+9y=f(x),$ where the Fourier series of the $4$-periodic forcing function is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}).\n$$\n\n*Hint: You may assume that the free term and all the cosine coefficients in the Fourier series of the particular solution are equal to zero.*\n\nThe Fourier series of a particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,{{field-1}}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2})\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We assume that a solution of our equation has the following Fourier series:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}).\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & ∼\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}))∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{b}_{n}n\\pi }{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}) \\\\ {y}_{p}^{″} & ∼\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{b}_{n}n\\pi }{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}))∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-\\frac{{b}_{n}{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the expressions for ${y}_{p}^{″}$ and ${y}_{p}$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-\\frac{{b}_{n}{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2})+9({\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}))={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}).\n$$\n\nRearranging the terms to find a common denominator:\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}(9-\\frac{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}{4})\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2})={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2})\n$$\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}(\\frac{36-{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}{4})\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2})={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}).\n$$\n\nNow, equating the sine coefficients:\n\n$$\n\\frac{{b}_{n}(36-{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2})}{4}=\\frac{2(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}\\pi }\\,⇒\\,{b}_{n}=\\frac{8(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}\\pi (36-{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2})}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,\\frac{8(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}\\pi (36-{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2})}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}).\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "1f09e7df-3236-5077-9169-9cb1ad13c54e" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186525093}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186525085 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186397701 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "60cf0d89-49e8-5b67-80c1-e7d2d7a3ffb4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{x}{8\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397703} {:answer/id #uuid "a1876296-7211-55be-aba4-bfdcc1a01df6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{x}{16\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397704} {:answer/id #uuid "09aff7b4-dbdd-58ab-8229-b25ef4b4ba18" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{x}{4\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397705} {:answer/id #uuid "fe23c02e-5cbf-556d-b7f3-55b796e71bfc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{x}{8\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397706}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186397706} :answer-field/id #uuid "ef63660a-274c-5dd7-9740-8afb5e0d6094" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397702} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1b222e16-92bf-5e7b-a075-99b8093081a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "n=4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397708} {:answer/id #uuid "e3cd2371-beae-59d9-8d7e-94aae5b22c28" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "n=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397709} {:answer/id #uuid "964c305f-0261-5625-959d-d4db38c740ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "n=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397710} {:answer/id #uuid "63032ea2-0b98-59b3-8f8b-f8122510525d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "n=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397711}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186397710} :answer-field/id #uuid "9f3873a4-d6a5-52d5-8df1-9961c00d5836" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397707}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "dd94ef93-5daf-566f-92be-7aef938749ca" :question/math-academy-id "q-343899" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation ${y}^{″}+{\\pi }^{2}y=f(x),$ where the $4$-periodic forcing function is given by \n\n$$\nf(x)∼5+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{n}^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}).\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options in the reasoning below regarding the resonant component of a particular solution of the equation.\n\nThe natural frequency of the system matches the frequency of the {{field-1}} harmonic of the forcing function. \n\nConsequently, the particular solution contains a resonant term of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{{p}_{2}}={{field-2}}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We begin by identifying resonance. The differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+{\\pi }^{2}y=f(x)\n$$\n\nhas a natural frequency of \n\n$$\n\\omega =\\sqrt{{\\pi }^{2}}=\\pi .\n$$\n\nThe frequencies of the forcing harmonics for a $4$-periodic function $(L=2)$ are \n\n$$\n{\\omega }_{n}=\\frac{n\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nFor $n=2$, we have ${\\omega }_{2}=\\pi .$ Since ${\\omega }_{2}=\\omega ,$ the $n=2$ harmonic of the forcing function causes resonance.\n\nFor the $n=2$ harmonic, the forcing term is \n\n$$\n{f}_{2}(x)=\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x).\n$$\n\nBecause this frequency matches the natural frequency, we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{{p}_{2}}=x(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)).\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{{p}_{2}}^{′} & =(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x))+x(-\\pi A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)+\\pi B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x)) \\\\ {y}_{{p}_{2}}^{″} & =-2\\pi A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)+2\\pi B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x)-{\\pi }^{2}x(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the expressions for ${y}_{{p}_{2}}^{″}$ and ${y}_{{p}_{2}}$ into the equation ${y}^{″}+{\\pi }^{2}y=\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x):$\n\n$$\n[-2\\pi A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)+2\\pi B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x)-{\\pi }^{2}{y}_{{p}_{2}}]+{\\pi }^{2}{y}_{{p}_{2}}=\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x)\n$$\n\nThe resonant terms involving $x$ cancel out, leaving\n\n$$\n-2\\pi A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)+2\\pi B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x)=\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x).\n$$\n\nNow, equating the sine and cosine coefficients, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-2\\pi A &=0 \\\\ 2\\pi B &=\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}A &=0 \\\\ B &=\\frac{1}{8\\pi }\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the resonant component of a particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{{p}_{2}}=\\frac{x}{8\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x).\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "3917470a-f482-5821-99c5-2f8790e885a5"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186525093 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186525016 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4e4cc218-73fe-5998-8ccf-bc16f3b90e09" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{8(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{3}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+20)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524514} {:answer/id #uuid "b9355ba0-889c-5450-99e3-818ab0a4f2f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{8(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+20)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524676} {:answer/id #uuid "d0a1ae82-6bda-5f83-b1fc-96118fbd6f97" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{8(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{3}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+20)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524728} {:answer/id #uuid "e570c1cd-6353-5178-af72-bcd3aab8e4d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{2(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{3}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+20)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524783}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524514} :answer-field/id #uuid "8f9455a9-07ca-5997-b4ca-16fa2b6ab07c" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524903} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "35c42206-68dd-51fc-99b6-ae210d259dac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524482} {:answer/id #uuid "3b2f4c1a-00a1-55c0-9e44-6bcc54f3f7de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524488} {:answer/id #uuid "8c0a8364-45bf-574e-bb9d-0ac0a859448d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524603} {:answer/id #uuid "cf0bdb29-be00-5a7a-ae5b-f5917ddd3a3f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524725}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524482} :answer-field/id #uuid "e37207bf-0563-5832-ad30-c6a23c3c930c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524938}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "9b306e59-ac03-5a23-8563-3ae450882f03" :question/math-academy-id "q-343882" :question/problem "Select the missing parts in a particular solution of the differential equation ${y}^{″}-5y=f(x),$ where the Fourier series of the $4$-periodic forcing function is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)∼5+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{3}\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}).\n$$\n\n*Hint: You may assume that all the cosine coefficients in the Fourier series of the particular solution are equal to zero.*\n\nThe Fourier series of a particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}∼{{field-1}}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,{{field-2}}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2})\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We assume that a solution of our equation has the following Fourier series:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}).\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & ∼\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}))∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{b}_{n}n\\pi }{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}) \\\\ {y}_{p}^{″} & ∼\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{b}_{n}n\\pi }{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}))∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-\\frac{{b}_{n}{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the expressions for ${y}_{p}^{″}$ and ${y}_{p}$ into the equation and have\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-\\frac{{b}_{n}{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2})-5(\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}))=5+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{3}\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}).\n$$\n\nRearranging the terms, we get \n\n$$\n-5\\cdot \\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}(-\\frac{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}{4}-5)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2})=5+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{3}\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}).\n$$\n\nNow, equating the free terms and sine coefficients, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-5\\cdot \\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}=5 \\\\ {b}_{n}(-\\frac{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}{4}-5)=\\frac{2(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{3}\\pi }\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}=-1 \\\\ {b}_{n}=-\\frac{8(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{3}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+20)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}∼-1+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,-\\frac{8(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{3}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+20)}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}).\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "52d5eeed-9b84-5f58-91ed-53e58dd47c9d" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186525122}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186525122 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524971 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "344d0e42-cd70-5ad8-90fc-7db764816086" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524478} {:answer/id #uuid "86854eda-5a8f-50ea-94b0-dd55b64266ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524590} {:answer/id #uuid "ce713a1f-b2ec-53d9-97c8-17515419704d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524724} {:answer/id #uuid "ed1a145b-0c0c-558c-963e-71228c0de2a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524800}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524590} :answer-field/id #uuid "04a2bae6-aeac-5a01-8bbe-2da7affb6702" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524842} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05e74e00-5bf0-5bcb-9c73-5ac12785eb89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{20(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{3}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+40)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524390} {:answer/id #uuid "20391762-3990-5977-9275-05105acc9764" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{20(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+40)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524440} {:answer/id #uuid "96006c5e-26f2-501a-82c3-1c733fab3f3f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{3}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+40)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524620} {:answer/id #uuid "ae2ce446-0043-5cc6-9d9f-038477aa3b6a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{20(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{3}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+40)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524662}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524662} :answer-field/id #uuid "3b8556e9-1a54-5d0b-a5a5-5efa77eeb553" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524866}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "234b7e73-d01a-5fb8-b7f2-7b7733987672" :question/math-academy-id "q-343886" :question/problem "Select the missing parts in a particular solution of the differential equation ${y}^{″}-10y=f(x),$ where the Fourier series of the $4$-periodic forcing function is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)∼10+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{5(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{3}\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}).\n$$\n\n*Hint: You may assume that all the cosine coefficients in the Fourier series of the particular solution are equal to zero.*\n\nThe Fourier series of a particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}∼{{field-1}}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,{{field-2}}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2})\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We assume that a solution of our equation has the following Fourier series:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}).\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & ∼\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}))∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{b}_{n}n\\pi }{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}) \\\\ {y}_{p}^{″} & ∼\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{b}_{n}n\\pi }{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}))∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-\\frac{{b}_{n}{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the expressions for ${y}_{p}^{″}$ and ${y}_{p}$ into the equation and have\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-\\frac{{b}_{n}{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2})-10(\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}))=10+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{5(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{3}\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}).\n$$\n\nRearranging the terms, we get \n\n$$\n-10\\cdot \\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}(-\\frac{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}{4}-10)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2})=10+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{5(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{3}\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}).\n$$\n\nNow, equating the free terms and sine coefficients, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-10\\cdot \\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}=10 \\\\ {b}_{n}(-\\frac{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}{4}-10)=\\frac{5(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{3}\\pi }\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}=-1 \\\\ {b}_{n}=\\frac{20(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{3}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+40)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}∼-1+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,\\frac{20(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{3}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+40)}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}).\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "a80e8457-da19-59dc-ac4a-260518248f13" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186525075}}] :activity/title "Solving ODEs Using Fourier Series" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524369}]}
